.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc

.venv/
venv/

dut-dashboard/frontend/node_modules/
dut-dashboard/frontend/dist/

dut-dashboard/logs/analyzer_output/
dut-dashboard/logs/snapshots.jsonl
dut-dashboard/logs/snapshots-*.jsonl
dut-dashboard/logs/duts.json
dut-dashboard/logs/dut-session-*.log
dut-dashboard/logs/dut-session-*/
dut-dashboard/logs/dut-session-*.zip
dut-dashboard/logs/.mplconfig/
# Captured DUT context: site surveys (P68) plus the connect-time snapshots and
# per-log bundles added in P73. All runtime capture data, never committed.
dut-dashboard/logs/site-surveys/
dut-dashboard/logs/context/
dut-dashboard/logs/context-bundles/

# Workspace module runtime state (SQLite DB + uploaded files)
dut-dashboard/data/

# Claude Code per-machine state. launch.json IS tracked so everyone gets working
# run configs; settings.local.json is not — it accumulates one entry per approved
# command, including absolute scratch paths and DUT credentials typed into curl.
# Never commit it. The `**/` matters: a pattern containing a slash is anchored to
# this file's directory, so a bare `.claude/...` would leave any nested copy
# (e.g. dut-dashboard/.claude/) unignored for anyone whose personal global
# gitignore does not already cover it.
**/.claude/settings.local.json
# Environment configuration. `.env` holds role passcodes and the DUT's own
# management credentials; `.env.example` is the committed inventory and carries
# no values. data/role-passcodes.env is already covered by data/ above.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
!.env.example

**/.claude/settings.json

# Firmware images staged for an upgrade. Vendor build artifacts, ~34 MB per model
# and five models per release, so they must never reach the history. Both types
# are covered: signed bundles for the web-UI transport, encrypted images for the
# management API.
test_*-signed/
*.tar.gz.sig
*-encrypt_*.bin

# Verification screenshots. Every phase leaves a few (preview/Playwright captures
# proving a layout or a fix), and by convention they land in the root of the repo
# under test -- here and in dut-dashboard/. Eleven of them reached CPU_Plots in
# #103 because nothing stopped a `git add -A`.
#
# Anchored and NON-recursive on purpose. A bare `*.png` would also swallow a real
# asset added later under frontend/public/ or docs/, and the failure would be
# silent: `git add` says nothing, the file never ships, and the build breaks
# somewhere else. These two lines cover exactly the drop points and leave every
# nested directory to behave normally. (The repo tracks no image of any kind
# today, so nothing existing is affected.)
/*.png
/dut-dashboard/*.png

# Serial captures. Unlike the screenshots above, these are ignored EVERYWHERE and
# legitimate fixtures are allowlisted back in. The two defaults point opposite
# ways on purpose, because the two mistakes cost different amounts: miss a new
# fixture and a test fails, which somebody sees within the hour; miss a capture
# and third-party identifiers are published, which nobody can take back. A
# capture of a flash session carries the DUT's real MAC addresses -- 34 distinct
# ones in each of the two large p72b files -- and this repository is public.
#
# Enumerating drop points was tried here first and was wrong. `dut-dashboard/
# logs/` is NOT ignored wholesale: it lists eleven specific patterns, so
# `dut-dashboard/logs/p72b-flash.log` stayed committable -- the most natural
# place of all to save one.
#
# The exception is the fixture subtree rather than one filename, because
# wifi_timeseries already tracks a marked capture and more are plausible.
# Anything outside it needs `git add -f`, which is friction pointing the right
# way.
*.log
!dut-dashboard/backend/tests/fixtures/**/*.log

# The demo kit's DOM verifier declares its own single dependency.
dut-dashboard/demo/verify/node_modules/
